{
  "metadata": {
    "description": "Synthetic university course timetabling stress-test dataset.",
    "seed": 42,
    "num_rooms": 10,
    "num_professors": 15,
    "num_courses": 40,
    "num_timeslots": 45,
    "notes": [
      "Only 3 of 10 rooms are type 'Lab' (capacities 30/24/20); 9 of 40 courses require 'Lab'. Raw room-hour supply isn't the bottleneck here - the bind is that most Lab courses share a professor pool overlapping with the two overloaded professors below, and Lab capacities (20-30) are tight against several Lab courses' enrollment (e.g. CS101L=28, CS102L=29), so capacity-respecting placements are a small subset of all room/time combinations.",
      "Professors P03 and P11 are each assigned 5 courses (one per department) AND carry 13-14 unavailable timeslots (vs. 3-6 for other professors) - the sharpest real bottleneck: each has under 70% of the week's 45 slots even theoretically free, and needs non-overlapping windows for 5 different courses (2 sessions/week each for 4 of them).",
      "Only LEC-A and LEC-B have capacity >= 150; four courses (CS101, MATH101, PHYS101, BUS101 - all enrollment >= 130) require 'Lecture' and must each get 2-3 sessions/week, competing for the same 2 rooms.",
      "10 corequisite relationships must never have overlapping sessions across any pair - including one cross-listed non-lab pair (MATH101/MATH201) as an edge case your algorithm shouldn't assume is always a lecture+lab pair.",
      "Courses with sessions_per_week > 1 require that many NON-overlapping (day,time) sessions in the output schedule, each a separate entry sharing the same course_id."
    ]
  },
